No. Even though the warp starts converged, `__activemask()` inside the branch is only a snapshot of currently active lanes, not a guarantee that all 19 branch participants are present. On Volta or newer, a caller’s mask can exclude lane 18, making the requested source value undefined.

For `__shfl_sync`, each caller must have its own bit set in the mask, and every non-exited thread named in that mask must execute the corresponding intrinsic with the same mask. The source lane must actively participate in that shuffle; `width = 32` makes source index 18 refer to physical lane 18, but does not make that lane participate. The intrinsic synchronizes the named participants for register exchange, not memory ordering.

Compute the participation mask before diverging, then use that fixed mask. This device function requires every lane of a full physical warp to call it at the converged point; each lane supplies its own float. Lanes 0–18 receive lane 18’s float, while lanes 19–31 retain their own value and never call the shuffle.

```cpp
__device__ float read_lane18(float value)
{
    const unsigned int thread_rank =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = thread_rank % 32u;
    const bool participates = lane <= 18u;
    const unsigned int mask =
        __ballot_sync(0xffffffffu, participates);

    if (participates) {
        value = __shfl_sync(mask, value, 18, 32);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Compile as CUDA device code and call only from complete warps, with all 32 lanes reaching the ballot. The ballot produces `0x0007ffff`; all 19 named lanes execute the same shuffle, including source lane 18. No separate `__syncwarp()`, shared memory, or atomics are needed.
